WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.720
You probably know Luigi as the most famous
sidekick in gaming history.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:05.749
A second fiddle.

00:00:05.749 --> 00:00:09.350
The guy who's always destined to be Player
2.

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:13.190
Luigi spends so much time in his older brother's
shadow, it's easy to forget that he's a fully

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:14.799
fledged character on his own.

00:00:14.799 --> 00:00:19.100
And, depending on the media he's appearing
in, not a particularly nice one.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:23.199
Here's a look at the most terrible things
Luigi has ever done.

00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:24.800
Just in it for the cash

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:29.789
In 1986, Nintendo and Grouper Studios released
an hour-long anime produced to promote the

00:00:29.789 --> 00:00:36.090
Japanese release of Super Mario Bros. 2, later
known to American gamers as The Lost Levels.

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:40.250
While the movie is a reasonably faithful adaptation
of the first couple Mario games, there's one

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:45.090
big difference: in the film, Luigi is the
absolute worst, and we're not just talking

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:47.540
about his bizarrely mis-colored costume.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:52.640
While Mario's motives are noble, Luigi only
agrees to join him on the quest after being

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:56.629
promised all the gold coins they find on the
adventure.

00:00:56.629 --> 00:01:01.680
As a result, he spends the whole cartoon greedily
searching for gold, even at the risk of his

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.290
friends' lives.

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:06.020
This version of Luigi isn't just greedy, either.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:09.830
While tripping on mushrooms, he punches Mario
in the face.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:15.200
And when the group runs low on food, Luigi
even thinks about eating Kibidango, the Mario

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.830
Bros.' dog-like companion.

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.660
Yikes, dude.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:20.190
Mad with power

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:25.250
Over in Germany, gamers were treated to Club
Nintendo, a magazine filled with absolutely

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:27.470
insane comic strips.

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:31.750
One of the most jarring is Super Mario: In
the Flush of Speed, where Luigi becomes a

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:36.270
cop and uses his newfound authority to make
Mario's life a living hell.

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:41.330
First, he pulls Mario over for going too fast
— apparently, when he's riding Yoshi down

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:45.380
the city streets, Mario needs to obey all
the regular traffic laws.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:51.890
Next, Luigi scolds Mario for referring to
him as Luigi and not "Officer Luigi," as befits

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:53.030
his new position.

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:58.090
Finally, when Mario hops off Yoshi's back
by spin-jumping — which, let's remember,

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:03.250
is the only way to dismount the dinosaur in
Super Mario World — Luigi informs his brother

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:07.030
that spin-jumps are strictly prohibited between
1 and 3 p.m.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:12.090
And while Luigi's a stickler for the rules,
he's not corrupt: he fines Mario — his own

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:15.250
brother — 60 coins for his transgressions.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:21.230
That's right: family means nothing in Officer
Luigi's totalitarian dystopia.

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:23.060
All talk

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:28.160
In Paper Mario: The Thousand-Year Door, Luigi
is revealed to be a lying blowhard, as it

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:33.230
turns out all the supposed adventures he boasts
about throughout the game are complete fiction.

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:38.330
For example , Luigi says that, in order to
defeat a monster, Luigi threw the squid-like

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:41.200
Blooper named Blooey at the boss' weak spot.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:46.080
According to Blooey, that's true, but Luigi
conveniently leaves out the part where he

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.610
missed, sending Blooey flying into a puddle
of lava.

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:54.350
Even worse, the general public actually buys
his propaganda, as books based on Luigi's

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:56.600
adventures begin appearing in the game.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:01.440
Though, given how little credit he gets for
his real adventures, it's hard to blame him.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.100
Totally uninhibited

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:09.180
In Super Paper Mario, Luigi isn't just comedic
relief — he's also the game's main villain.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:14.100
According to the Dark Prognosticus, a book
of prophecies written by an unknown author,

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:19.650
the "man in green" is the perfect host for
the Chaos Heart, which will destroy the world.

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:22.460
That man in green is, of course, Luigi.

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:27.790
After being captured by the master hypnotist
Nastasia, Luigi's inhibitions are released

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:34.340
and he becomes the sinister Mr. L. Openly
jealous of Mario's abilities, Mr. L teams

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:37.680
up with a new robot buddy and spends the game
attacking Mario.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:46.709
He even makes a move on Princess Peach!

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:51.610
And that's even before the mercenary Dimentio
fuses with Luigi and tries to bring about

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:54.220
the end of the world using the Chaos Heart.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:56.210
So was it all mind control?

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:59.540
Or did the bad guys just unleash Luigi's true
feelings?

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:02.470
All we can say for sure is: watch your back,
Mario.

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:04.050
Fraternal abuse

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:08.650
Most of the time, Luigi's love-hate relationship
with his brother ends up coming across as

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:10.010
passive-aggressive.

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:14.450
But in Mario and Luigi: Bowser's Inside Story,
there's nothing passive about it.

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:18.820
Early in the game, both brothers get hammers,
which they can use in combat and to solve

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:19.850
puzzles.

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:24.270
While Mario uses his hammer in reasonable
ways, like destroying obstacles that block

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:28.460
his path, Luigi uses his to beat on his older
brother.

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:33.500
Pummeling Mario is the only way to proceed
in the game, but you better believe it hurts.

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:39.240
One of the main reasons to use Luigi's hammer
is to transform Mario into Mini-Mario — y'know,

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:43.690
the form Mario takes when he's damaged by
an enemy, leaving him unable to break bricks

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:45.420
and teetering on the brink of death.

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:49.770
But hey, it also allows Mario to pass through
small passages, so why not gamble with your

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:50.770
brother's life?

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:54.040
Looks like Luigi is working out some serious
issues here.

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:59.060
Tellingly, Luigi doesn't even flinch when
he learns he has to hit Mario on the head.

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:00.169
That's not a loving brother.

00:05:00.169 --> 00:05:02.199
That's a cold-hearted menace.

00:05:02.199 --> 00:05:03.460
Thanks for watching!

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:05.930
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:07.910
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

